      "headline": "Mexico's Judiciary reforms transparency and public information rules",
      "context": "The Judicial Administration Board has amended its transparency and public-information agreement, a formal action published in the Official Federal Gazette.",
      "background": "Mexico's judicial system underwent fundamental restructuring in 2024, replacing appointment with election for much of the federal judiciary, the new Supreme Court took office in September 2025, and practical tests now center on case resolution speed, enforcement, and independence when government or powerful interests are involved.",
      "view": "The amendment formalizes transparency and data-protection procedures for the reformed judiciary. Publication in the Official Federal Gazette makes the new rules binding on federal courts. The substance of the changes is not disclosed in available sources, so the practical effect on case disclosure, financial filings, or judicial independence cannot be determined from this announcement alone.",
      "prediction": "Courts will apply the revised rules to information requests and personnel records immediately. Whether the amendment strengthens or weakens public access depends on specific provisions not yet detailed in public reporting, that detail will emerge when transparency organizations or media test the new procedures against specific cases or records requests.",
